## BLOCKER 1 — f_mkfs API version gate

The f_fdisk version gate already exists. Apply the same gate to every f_mkfs call.

R0.14b and earlier (5-argument form):
  f_mkfs("0:", FM_FAT32, 0, fmtWork, sizeof(fmtWork));
  f_mkfs("1:", FM_FAT32, 0, fmtWork, sizeof(fmtWork));

R0.15 and later (4-argument form, MKFS_PARM struct):
  const MKFS_PARM mp = {FM_FAT32, 0, 0, 0, 0};
  f_mkfs("0:", &mp, fmtWork, sizeof(fmtWork));
  f_mkfs("1:", &mp, fmtWork, sizeof(fmtWork));

The version check is the same one already required beside f_fdisk. Both checks
read from the same ff.c version string — do them once, apply to both calls.
Record the confirmed version in a comment beside both f_fdisk and f_mkfs.

## BLOCKER 2 — MX_FATFS_Init() must not mount any volume

Remove all f_mount calls from MX_FATFS_Init(). It initialises the FATFS objects
and disk layer only. No mounting occurs there.

Add a single custom function to fatfs.c:
  FRESULT sdMountAll(void);

sdMountAll() performs in order:
  1. Attempt f_mount(&FatFS, "0:", 1)
  2. Attempt f_mount(&SysCalFatFS, "1:", 1)
  3. If either returns FR_NO_FILESYSTEM, return FR_NO_FILESYSTEM to caller
  4. On both success, return FR_OK

main.c calls sdMountAll() after the first-boot detection block.
First-boot detection calls sdMountAll() and branches on FR_NO_FILESYSTEM.
After format, sdMountAll() is called again to mount the freshly created volumes.

This eliminates double-mount and partial-format ambiguity.

